ating is if they all  seem the same and an easy way for them  all to see is a scene the same  is if they are all aiming at the same  pg-13 demographic by having some that  are rated we get to spread it out we get  to show that they are more than just  this one thing and another way for  everything to see the same and have it  start to stay a is by having it all  under the same studio so beyond just the  fact that I want to acknowledge the  things that Fox had done and done right  that Disney would not do I also want to  address just the broad reasons that even  if that wasn't the case even if Fox had  done nothing but screw these movies up  for the last four or five films I still  wouldn't want Marvel to regain the  rights back and here's why  because at this point the only benefit  to Marvel having the rights bag is to  integrate the x-men into the MCU and I  get that a lot of people want that but  it's actually a good idea in my personal  opinion it really isn't so here's the  thing about x-men and the thing that is  a little bit weird about it  even though the x-men and I'm talking  about the comics now even though the  x-men exist in the Marvel Universe and  in the same space as Avengers and all  this other stuff they've kind of always  been kept in their own bubble and they  work best that way really in terms of  the comic books you can break down the  vast majority of Marvel's universe into  three categories Avengers heroes cosmic  heroes x-men heroes and for the most  part those three universes don't cross  over very much and when they do they  actually make a really big event out of  it it ends up being something like  infinity war where the cosmic heroes  come down to earth or in terms of x-men  crossing over with the rest of the  heroes you get something like x-men vs.  Avengers or House of M these big massive  events in the company itself seems to  acknowledge that the idea of everything  that goes on in the x-men because that  is a massive cast in and of itself  crossing over with the rest of the  Marvel Universe is a big deal because  they are so often kept separate part of  it is just the sheer volume of  characters the thing that's been  ridiculous about Sony looking to create  a whole cinematic universe around just  spider-man is that as ultimate  spider-man is and as great as the  supporting cast is it really all  revolves around that one single  character that's not the case with the  x-men it makes sense  that the x-men is resulting in spin-offs  and new media and all the stuff because  the cast of mutant characters is  absurdly huge so part of it is I'm just  not honestly sure if there is room in  one cinematic universe for the x-men to  actively be crossing over with Avengers  and I think if the Marvel were to  require the x-men and they were to say  that it's happening in the same universe  well you can't have to have them cross  over because they're all occupying the  same space the comics don't really  address the fact that you know the x-men  are dealing with these world-threatening  and comic book readers kind of accept it  on a we won't bring it up if you won't  basis I'm not sure mainstream audiences  will go for that if you do establish all  these people are occupying the same  space and if you're thinking that you  can just we'll just keep it separate for  the most part it's working with  guardians the galaxies and the cosmic  stuff that hasn't crossed over and been  an issue with the Avengers stuff yet but  that's because there's an inherent  natural barrier between the cosmic stuff  and the rest of the MCU which is that  it's in space it's physically very very  very far away so you actually have to go  out of your way to bring them together  as opposed to go out of your way to  explain why they have to be kept  separate which would be the case with  x-men and the MCU and then there's all  the logistical ideas of trying to  integrate the mutants into the MCU  because the whole thing that really  